1
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:10,533
[engine starts]

2
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:11,700
[revving]

3
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:17,966
[upbeat music playing]

4
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:24,000
[Jerry] What if, at just the right time,

5
00:00:24,066 --> 00:00:27,200
just the right people
made just the right thing?

6
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:32,799
What if it came out so right
that no matter how hard anybody tried,

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,266
they couldn't quite make a thing
that was better than that thing?

8
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:38,866
It doesn't happen much.

9
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:43,066
But the 1973 Porsche 911 Carrera RS...

10
00:00:43,699 --> 00:00:44,833
is one of those things.

11
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:54,633
It was the first time a car company
put a race car into production.

12
00:00:55,188 --> 00:00:58,391
Altogether, they made 1580 examples.

13
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,166
They had thinner glass, thinner metal.

14
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:06,666
The very first aerodynamic spoiler
ever put on any car sold to the public.

15
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,566
[engine revving]

16
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:11,733
It has a 210-horsepower flat-six engine.

17
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,666
And all of these numbers mean nothing.

18
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:19,066
You can ask anybody
that's ever driven one.

19
00:01:20,066 --> 00:01:24,100
There's something about this car
that feels perfect.

20
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:30,000
[phone ringing]

21
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:31,899
-[Seth] Hello?
-[Jerry] Seth Meyers?

22
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:33,966
-[Seth] Jerry Seinfeld?
-[Jerry] That's correct.

23
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:36,666
We had talked about having some coffee.
What do you think?

24
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:38,766
[Seth] I would never say no
to coffee with you.

25
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:40,566
[Jerry] That is what I like to hear.

26
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,966
Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld,

27
00:01:47,033 --> 00:01:50,133
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

28
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:54,899
Today, my guest is the beloved host of
"Weekend Update" on Saturday Night Live,

29
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:58,133
and the new host of
Late Night with Seth Meyers...

30
00:01:58,199 --> 00:01:59,399
Seth Meyers.

31
00:02:04,566 --> 00:02:06,399
[jazz music playing]

32
00:02:06,866 --> 00:02:07,833
Nice to see you.

33
00:02:07,894 --> 00:02:10,263
I'm excited for the reveal
of what kind of car we're driving.

34
00:02:10,533 --> 00:02:13,266
Well, it's a dead-guy car.
I bought it from a dead guy,

35
00:02:13,566 --> 00:02:15,966
and it's not getting sold again
until I'm dead.

36
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:18,833
This car doesn't change hands
without somebody dying.

37
00:02:19,466 --> 00:02:21,366
-Why did I pick this car for you?
-Why?

38
00:02:21,433 --> 00:02:23,633
-This car is no-nonsense.
-Perfect.

39
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:32,433
[Jerry] So, you, Adam Sandler,
and Sarah Silverman

40
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:35,133
are all from the same part
of New Hampshire?

41
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,033
[Seth] Yeah.

42
00:02:36,089 --> 00:02:38,091
[Jerry] Seth, Sarah, and Sandler,
same area.

43
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:39,025
[Seth] It's insane.

44
00:02:39,092 --> 00:02:42,328
When I was in high school, that was when
Sandler first started coming up.

45
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:45,666
I remember thinking, "I can't believe
a guy from my town is on SNL."

46
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:46,966
And then when I was in college,

47
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:49,969
Sarah was on, and I was like,
"I can't believe two people..."

48
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:52,372
At that point you think,
"There are not gonna be three."

49
00:02:55,266 --> 00:02:57,400
If I said something funny at school
that got a laugh,

50
00:02:57,466 --> 00:03:00,333
I would come home
and say someone else had said it.

51
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,448
So I didn't feel, um, self-aggrandizing.

52
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:04,200
Oh, my God.

53
00:03:04,266 --> 00:03:06,366
Because if I told them that I had said it,

54
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:10,066
they might have just, like,
polite laughed 'cause I'm their son.

55
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:13,593
Boy, you were really calculating
from the very beginning.

56
00:03:13,666 --> 00:03:15,366
My dad has long said,

57
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:18,366
"Never say a joke twice
in my conversation."

58
00:03:18,698 --> 00:03:20,900
Because sometimes you'll think
you'll say something funny

59
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:23,136
and you'll think no one heard it
so you'll say it again.

60
00:03:23,799 --> 00:03:24,933
He's like, "Never do that,

61
00:03:24,971 --> 00:03:28,174
'cause there's a chance everyone heard it
and they didn't like it the first time."

62
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:32,866
And nothing's worse than saying something
that's not funny twice.

63
00:03:33,166 --> 00:03:37,233
I love how your dad, the finance guy,
has all these comedy principles.

64
00:03:37,299 --> 00:03:39,833
Oh, my God.
So many more than I have.

65
00:03:40,899 --> 00:03:42,299
[jazz music playing]

66
00:03:44,691 --> 00:03:47,160
-[Jerry] So, what year were you born?
-[Seth] '73.

67
00:03:47,466 --> 00:03:50,633
-[Jerry] And this car is a '73.
-[Seth] That's pretty exciting.

68
00:03:50,797 --> 00:03:52,332
[Jerry] When I told you it was a '73,

69
00:03:52,398 --> 00:03:53,866
-it didn't... Wow.
-[Seth] Nothing.

70
00:03:53,933 --> 00:03:56,669
[Jerry] That is really
a special kind of "out of it."

71
00:03:56,736 --> 00:03:57,604
[Seth laughs]

72
00:03:59,238 --> 00:04:00,940
[Seth] That ever appeal to you,
motorcycles?

73
00:04:01,007 --> 00:04:01,841
[Jerry] Yes.

74
00:04:02,108 --> 00:04:05,078
That's like the cigarette
of transportation for me.

75
00:04:05,166 --> 00:04:08,933
The downside far outweighs
whatever upside.

76
00:04:09,666 --> 00:04:12,066
-How many cars do you have?
-An amount that,

77
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:15,299
if you looked at it, you would not go,
"Well, that makes sense."

78
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:17,533
I get that.

79
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,566
[Seth] The summer stresses me out.

80
00:04:22,633 --> 00:04:24,800
Because everybody gets so excited
about the summer

81
00:04:24,866 --> 00:04:27,033
and I kind of just don't get it.

82
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:29,633
People are like,
"How can you be inside all day?"

83
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:33,000
Whereas I wanna say like,
"Everything I wanna do is inside."

84
00:04:35,808 --> 00:04:38,411
[Jerry] If you give someone a 50,
it's a big thing now,

85
00:04:38,478 --> 00:04:40,113
-where they check it quite a bit.
-Yeah.

86
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:43,700
And it's like,
"Well, I used it. Why can't you use it?"

87
00:04:43,766 --> 00:04:48,266
We're all just kind of going along
with this "paper has value" scam.

88
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:49,500
-Right?
-Yes.

89
00:04:49,566 --> 00:04:52,700
So, why don't we just go along with it?
Why are you stopping me?

90
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:01,866
[Seth] We were doing this show
at the Chicago Improv Festival.

91
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:04,666
And so that's how SNL first saw me.

92
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:06,700
Tell me about that phone call.

93
00:05:06,773 --> 00:05:07,907
Somebody I knew said,

94
00:05:07,974 --> 00:05:11,577
"SNL called and they want you to send them
an audition tape."

95
00:05:11,933 --> 00:05:13,466
I remember being incredibly flattered,

96
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:15,866
but thinking that that would be
the end of the anecdote.

97
00:05:15,933 --> 00:05:17,766
Always thought
the end of the anecdote would be,

98
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:20,700
"And that was the day
SNL gave me their address."

99
00:05:21,621 --> 00:05:24,624
-[Jerry] How many years you been there?
-[Seth] Just finished my 12th season.

100
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:26,366
-[Jerry] Holy cow.
-[Seth] I know.

101
00:05:26,433 --> 00:05:32,333
There have been a few people on the show
that Lorne has let me tell we're hiring.

102
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:34,700
-Taran Killam, who's a cast member.
-[Jerry] Yeah.

103
00:05:34,766 --> 00:05:37,599
[Seth] I was flying to LA.,
and Taran was on my flight.

104
00:05:37,666 --> 00:05:39,766
I was in business class
and he was in coach.

105
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:43,233
And I walked back and I told him,
I'm like, "Just five years.

106
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,099
All you gotta do is wait five years,
and you'll be in business class."

107
00:05:46,166 --> 00:05:48,633
-Except that's not really true either.
-It's not true at all.

108
00:05:49,366 --> 00:05:50,800
[jazz music playing]

109
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:57,233
I don't really wanna leave my car here.

110
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:01,333
I don't like this spot,
in front of this pickup with a bullet hole

111
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:02,833
-in the windshield.
-[Seth laughs]

112
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:06,599
It'll be watched over by this
skull-faced woman on the horse.

113
00:06:07,467 --> 00:06:09,435
[Jerry] Know how unhappy
this makes a guy like me?

114
00:06:09,599 --> 00:06:11,200
I'm delighted to see it firsthand.

115
00:06:12,772 --> 00:06:14,407
[no audible dialogue]

116
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:21,547
-[Jerry] That seem low?
-[Seth] Shouldn't touch the ground. Right?

117
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:23,566
-Now, I'm not a car guy.
-But even you...

118
00:06:34,627 --> 00:06:36,929
-I would love a coffee.
-I would love a coffee, yes.

119
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,599
-[waitress] Americano?
-Americano's perfect.

120
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:40,333
I'll try the French press.

121
00:06:48,574 --> 00:06:50,977
We crossed paths at SNL,
when you came to do "Really!?!"

122
00:06:51,066 --> 00:06:52,200
-Right? That was--
-Yeah.

123
00:06:52,266 --> 00:06:55,566
Which was the most fun thing
I've ever done on that show.

124
00:06:55,633 --> 00:06:58,533
The story has revealed many details
about Massa's behavior

125
00:06:58,584 --> 00:07:00,219
and brings us to a segment we like to call

126
00:07:00,286 --> 00:07:02,288
"Really!?! With Seth and Jerry."

127
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:03,933
[applause and cheering]

128
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,799
I remember being on the phone
with you and pitching,

129
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:09,700
"Do you think
you'd wanna do a 'Really!?!'"

130
00:07:09,833 --> 00:07:10,833
Remember what you said?

131
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,000
Yeah, I said, "I think I can.
In fact, I think I invented it."

132
00:07:14,133 --> 00:07:17,333
And, Eric, when you finally admitted
you groped a male staffer,

133
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:20,033
you insisted it was
just during a tickle fight.

134
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:22,133
Really?

135
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:23,433
[laughter]

136
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:28,700
I've always heard that
the political matrix of that show

137
00:07:28,766 --> 00:07:30,766
is extremely difficult to navigate.

138
00:07:30,833 --> 00:07:32,700
Politically, I'm at the top of the show,

139
00:07:32,766 --> 00:07:34,700
so maybe that's why
I think it's not that political.

140
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:35,766
The way the president is like,

141
00:07:35,833 --> 00:07:38,066
"No, I think this is about
as well as it could run."

142
00:07:38,566 --> 00:07:40,433
[jazz music playing]

143
00:07:43,366 --> 00:07:46,266
How often do you
have to do charity events?

144
00:07:46,333 --> 00:07:48,733
Let me explain to you something
about charity events.

145
00:07:48,799 --> 00:07:50,533
There are people that would say

146
00:07:50,599 --> 00:07:53,500
that your whole career
is an act of charity.

147
00:07:55,466 --> 00:08:00,299
That you devote yourself
to this somewhat painful existence

148
00:08:00,366 --> 00:08:02,500
to make the world a little brighter.

149
00:08:03,015 --> 00:08:04,717
The first time my friends
see me do stand-up,

150
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:06,700
they all talk about how nervous they are.

151
00:08:06,752 --> 00:08:10,923
When they actually see how minimal it is.
It's like, "He's gonna talk for an hour."

152
00:08:10,990 --> 00:08:13,826
And once they've told me that, now,
that's what I think every time I walk out.

153
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:16,166
Like, that's my last thought is,
I'm like, "An hour."

154
00:08:16,233 --> 00:08:18,299
It's like that thing I'll remember
at the last minute,

155
00:08:18,366 --> 00:08:20,233
-how far the jump is.
-Mm-hmm.

156
00:08:20,299 --> 00:08:23,666
It's kind of like, "What if I'm at Macy's
and I get diarrhea, what will I do?"

157
00:08:23,733 --> 00:08:26,133
-Right.
-You find that doesn't happen that often.

158
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:28,599
-You shouldn't plan your life around it.
-No, exactly.

159
00:08:28,666 --> 00:08:31,433
You shouldn't go through your life
wearing an adult diaper, just in case...

160
00:08:32,233 --> 00:08:34,100
you get diarrhea at Macy's.

161
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:36,966
So you'll take some jokes
from Update, or...

162
00:08:37,033 --> 00:08:41,199
I close with jokes we've cut from Update
because of NBC censors.

163
00:08:41,333 --> 00:08:44,533
-But they're just killers.
-As Rodney Dangerfield used to say to me,

164
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,466
"The killers are wanted in all 50 states."

165
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:51,199
I met Don Rickles for the first time.

166
00:08:51,266 --> 00:08:52,699
He was sitting at a table alone.

167
00:08:52,766 --> 00:08:55,000
And I was like,
"I love him. I gotta say hi."

168
00:08:55,066 --> 00:08:57,033
-Right.
-So I knelt down and I was like,

169
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,300
"Mr. Rickles, my name is Seth Meyers,
I'm a huge fan."

170
00:08:59,366 --> 00:09:02,233
He was drinking coffee, he looked at me,
rolled his eyes,

171
00:09:02,933 --> 00:09:04,266
took a sip of his coffee...

172
00:09:05,511 --> 00:09:07,613
and then said,
"You're gonna have to give me a second,

173
00:09:07,699 --> 00:09:09,666
I'm not usually this excited
to meet someone."

174
00:09:10,182 --> 00:09:12,852
He said, uh, "I'm sorry to hear
Saturday Night Live got canceled."

175
00:09:13,152 --> 00:09:15,588
And I go, "It didn't get canceled."
He goes, "A guy can dream."

176
00:09:16,966 --> 00:09:18,533
[jazz music playing]

177
00:09:20,833 --> 00:09:23,033
-Did you see Liberace on Sunday night?
-Yeah.

178
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:26,633
Did you see that thing,
that he sold out Radio City 56 nights?

179
00:09:26,699 --> 00:09:28,033
Fred Armisen from our show

180
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:31,066
was an usher at Radio City
Music Hall for those 50 shows.

181
00:09:31,699 --> 00:09:32,866
How crazy is that?

182
00:09:32,933 --> 00:09:35,766
But the thing he told me is that
the first night, Liberace was like,

183
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,699
"There's a couple here
who have been married 50 years.

184
00:09:38,766 --> 00:09:40,666
They met at a show of mine."

185
00:09:40,733 --> 00:09:42,633
Oh, no, I know what's coming.

186
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,833
Did it every night. Place would go crazy.

187
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:46,833
But that is that experience of happiness.

188
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,900
-Like, why not? Everybody's--
-Where's the victim?

189
00:09:48,966 --> 00:09:50,633
-Where's the victim?
-Yeah.

190
00:09:50,699 --> 00:09:53,633
Like, it's all... not true.

191
00:09:53,699 --> 00:09:55,166
There are people who think
that was a rip-off

192
00:09:55,233 --> 00:09:57,666
but I think that's just
the most wonderful part of the experience.

193
00:09:57,933 --> 00:09:59,466
[jazz music playing]

194
00:10:01,766 --> 00:10:04,300
-My dad is a great storyteller.
-What does he do?

195
00:10:04,366 --> 00:10:06,800
-He's just in finance.
-A funny guy in finance.

196
00:10:06,866 --> 00:10:10,533
My mom... Like, when he's trying to tell
a story, like, her interjections...

197
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:12,666
And the amount I've seen my dad
do this, like...

198
00:10:15,166 --> 00:10:18,466
It's like Kobe Bryant threads
a bounce pass to a lesser player.

199
00:10:18,533 --> 00:10:19,666
-Yeah.
-Just bounces...

200
00:10:19,719 --> 00:10:21,053
He's just constantly like...

201
00:10:21,933 --> 00:10:23,566
Like, "I'm better, I'm better than this."

202
00:10:23,900 --> 00:10:26,800
And they are desperately in love
with one another,

203
00:10:26,866 --> 00:10:29,866
but yet she cannot help but interrupt him.

204
00:10:30,133 --> 00:10:33,000
It would be like if somebody
loved, loved violin

205
00:10:33,066 --> 00:10:35,233
and while the violinist was playing,
kept walking and going,

206
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:36,333
"How great is this?"

207
00:10:37,699 --> 00:10:38,666
It's the best.

208
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,633
[saxophone playing]

209
00:10:47,733 --> 00:10:49,800
My first four years
on the show were rocky.

210
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:52,699
Like, "I might be, of everybody here,
the worst one at it.

211
00:10:53,033 --> 00:10:56,300
I might be the worst at impressions,
I might be the worst at accents.

212
00:10:56,355 --> 00:10:58,157
Maybe I should
just be a writer on the show.

213
00:10:58,224 --> 00:11:00,092
Maybe I just don't belong in this cast."

214
00:11:00,433 --> 00:11:02,466
I remember at my third year, my dad said,

215
00:11:02,533 --> 00:11:04,533
"I feel like what you need
to really break out

216
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,233
is, like, a couple more characters."

217
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:07,600
And I remember saying to him, like,

218
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,566
"You've done a very good job of being
a tough, honest critic with me.

219
00:11:12,633 --> 00:11:13,766
I no longer need it.

220
00:11:14,066 --> 00:11:17,100
So you have to shift
from what you've been doing

221
00:11:17,166 --> 00:11:19,533
to a completely different
role of support."

222
00:11:25,233 --> 00:11:27,633
A great thing my mom used to do
when I started on the show,

223
00:11:27,699 --> 00:11:30,766
was she made a highlight reel
of my time on the show.

224
00:11:30,833 --> 00:11:32,833
But if I had one line
in a scene as a waiter,

225
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,166
she'd just cut that out.

226
00:11:34,393 --> 00:11:36,729
People would come over and she'd go,
"Have you seen Seth on SNL?"

227
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:38,033
It would jump-cut to me being, like,

228
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:40,100
"Here, your dinner,"
then it would cut out.

229
00:11:40,566 --> 00:11:43,300
It'd be me dressed in a bear suit,
then it would cut out of that.

230
00:11:43,366 --> 00:11:45,466
I was like, "I think this is less of
a highlight reel,

231
